  "Question": "The set of solutions for the equation \\(\\log_{10}(a^2-15a)=2\\) consists of:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "two integers",
    "B": "one integer and one fraction",
    "C": "two irrational numbers",
    "D": "two non-real numbers",
    "E": "no numbers, that is, the set is empty"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "A base-10 logarithm equals \\(2\\) precisely when its positive argument equals \\(10^2=100\\). Therefore the equation is equivalent to\n\\[\na^2-15a=100.\n\\]\nMoving all terms to one side reveals a factorable quadratic:\n\\[\na^2-15a-100=(a-20)(a+5)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero only if at least one factor is zero, so the only candidates are \\(a=20\\) and \\(a=-5\\).\n\nBoth values make \\(a^2-15a=100>0\\), so both satisfy the original logarithmic equation. The solution set is \\(\\{-5,20\\}\\), consisting of two integers, choice A.",
